the holiday we observed monday was once  known as decoration day its origins  dating back to the american civil war  the earliest known observance took place  in charleston south carolina in 1865.  around 250 union soldiers who died in  confederate custody were buried in  unmarked graves to honor them recently  freed black residents of charleston  created a proper burial ground and days  later thousands came to decorate the  graves as we observed this memorial day  we turn to a remarkable portrait of the  harlem hell fighters on display at the  smithsonian art museum in washington  nancy chen talked to the artist behind  the piece  you see these nine  strong beautiful  young men looking like superstars this  is a snapshot of some of the brave men  who made up the  369th infantry regiment of world war one  that are known as the harlem hell  fighters  we visited bisa butler last summer at  her new jersey studio as she stitched  life into her largest portrait quilt  the fabrics carefully chosen to help  tell the story of the soldiers they're  from world war one and they came right  out of harlem  most of them were black and puerto rican  but even in war times the harlem health  fighters couldn't escape racism  segregation prevented them from serving  alongside white u.s soldiers it was the  french who gave them the opportunity to  fight the united states in essence  loaned the soldiers to the french and it  meant a lot for me to be able to portray  not only are these young men fighting  for their country but they fought for  their very dignity as men  the 369th infantry unit spent  191 days on the front lines that's more  time in combat than any other american  unit  their bravery mostly forgotten over a  hundred years later each of the nine  soldiers took a month so this took 11  months in total  but when you look at the piece and you  look at one figure you're seeing a whole  month of my life  put into making sure that every detail  was  accurate it jumps out at you  the dignity of the men they're staring  you right in the eye and compelling you  to see them for their humanity to see  their american patriotism in 1919 the  harlem health fighters came back home to  a victory parade in new york and despite  their bravery and patriotism they still  faced bigotry and discrimination it's a  portrait of these men but it's also a  window  and not just a window into the past but  a window into the soul a window into the  real vision  of who and what  and how african americans want to be  seen in this country  last year the u.s government awarded the  courageous soldiers the congressional  gold medal thank you to this body for  putting aside partisanship and helping  write a centuries-old wrong  it's never too late to do the right  thing it meant a lot for me to have this  piece here at the renwick which is right  next to the white house because it's  reframing where they belong and it's  giving them the acknowledgement that  they should have had for cbs saturday  morning nancy chen new york  a beautiful tribute the harlem  hellfighters portrait quilt as part of  the exhibition this present moment  crafting a better world showcasing over  a hundred artists
 

